WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:07.716
We have a pretty epic
water crisis in Mexico City.

00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:10.010
And so we figured,
let's start harvesting

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:12.679
rainwater in these neighborhoods
where people need it.

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:16.182
Isla Urbana is an organization
that designs and installs

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:18.268
rainwater harvesting systems.

00:00:18.268 --> 00:00:20.812
We've installed
something like 24,000

00:00:20.812 --> 00:00:23.481
or 25,000 rainwater
harvesting systems

00:00:23.481 --> 00:00:25.650
just using revenue.

00:00:35.285 --> 00:00:38.538
We once spent 25 days
without any water.

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:40.665
Only in the nighttime
we get some water,

00:00:40.665 --> 00:00:42.167
Friday or Saturday.

00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:46.171
It looks yellow, it smells.

00:00:46.171 --> 00:00:47.547
We have to buy drinking water.

00:00:48.006 --> 00:00:50.091
I tell my wife, this will be

00:00:52.552 --> 00:00:54.971
The rainwater harvesting
systems that we install

00:00:54.971 --> 00:00:56.014
are pretty simple.

00:00:56.014 --> 00:00:59.517
It teaches people
how to harvest, filter,

00:00:59.517 --> 00:01:01.728
and potabilize water at home.

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:03.938
The whole thing starts with
the roof of the house,

00:01:03.938 --> 00:01:05.065
which is the catchment surface.

00:01:05.065 --> 00:01:08.109
So we work with the family
so that they have a very clear

00:01:08.109 --> 00:01:10.570
understanding of keeping
the roof as clean as possible.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:12.572
The water that falls
off of that roof,

00:01:12.572 --> 00:01:14.699
we channel through
gutters and pipes.

00:01:14.699 --> 00:01:16.326
We chlorinate that water.

00:01:16.326 --> 00:01:18.203
We do a simple
sedimentation process

00:01:18.203 --> 00:01:20.246
that allows fine
particles of sediments

00:01:20.246 --> 00:01:21.748
to settle in the tank.

00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:23.792
And then after that,
we pass it through

00:01:23.792 --> 00:01:26.086
a pretty simple filtration system

00:01:26.086 --> 00:01:28.296
that then feeds water
to the rest of the house.

00:01:28.296 --> 00:01:31.591
And we can get water of
whatever quality we need.

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:33.593 position:63%
From kind of general use domestic water,

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:35.845
we can get purified
water that you could bottle.

00:01:35.845 --> 00:01:39.390
We prepare the roof when
the rainy season is near,

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:41.726
and there we collect the rain.

00:01:41.726 --> 00:01:45.647
It is useful for many things,
and it is very clean.

00:01:45.647 --> 00:01:50.568
We harvest from 15,000 to
20,000 liters of water per year.

00:01:50.568 --> 00:01:52.654
Ten people occupy that water.

00:01:52.654 --> 00:01:55.490
We spend the whole
year with that water.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.325
It is enough, thankfully.

00:01:57.325 --> 00:02:00.495
When the rainy
season was over,

00:02:00.495 --> 00:02:02.372
we would only have
800 liters of water left,

00:02:02.372 --> 00:02:06.084
and we had to make it
last three or four months.

00:02:06.084 --> 00:02:08.962 position:66%
Now with this rainwater harvesting system,

00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:12.632
we can capture
3,000 liters of rainwater.

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:17.632
It has certain filters that
make the water better quality.

00:02:18.138 --> 00:02:19.222
Harvesting rainwater,

00:02:19.222 --> 00:02:20.306
it's an ancient practice

00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:23.643
that people have done forever
and kind of largely abandoned

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:25.603
as we kind of
adopted other ideas

00:02:25.603 --> 00:02:27.272
of how we should get water

00:02:27.272 --> 00:02:29.399
and what modern water
access should look like.

00:02:29.399 --> 00:02:31.109
But in that pursuit,

00:02:31.109 --> 00:02:33.486
there's all of these

00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:35.780
that just kind of fell through
the cracks and were left in these

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:37.115
continuously
precarious situations,

00:02:37.115 --> 00:02:41.536
living with often very, very
small amounts of water,

00:02:41.536 --> 00:02:43.538
often with water
very poor quality,

00:02:43.538 --> 00:02:45.290
often paying an
enormous amount

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:47.709
proportional to their
income, getting water.

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:50.295
Well, the scarcity has
been going on for years.

00:02:50.295 --> 00:02:53.381
We no longer receive water daily.

00:02:53.381 --> 00:02:56.092
It recently failed us
for like 15 days.

00:02:56.092 --> 00:02:57.927
We had to buy a pipe,

00:02:57.927 --> 00:03:00.763
it cost us 700 pesos.

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:04.726
They were increasingly
limiting our water service.

00:03:04.726 --> 00:03:08.271
The water from the faucet,
well, it's not that bad,

00:03:08.271 --> 00:03:11.983 position:56%
but it has leaves, dirt, bacteria.
It's no longer good for drinking.
We have spent 25 days without water.

00:03:11.983 --> 00:03:14.277
It's no longer good for drinking.

00:03:14.277 --> 00:03:17.322
Mexico City is one
of the top 10 cities

00:03:17.322 --> 00:03:19.199
rated most likely to
run out of water

00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:21.451
in the world by
The Nature Conservancy.

00:03:21.451 --> 00:03:24.704
We've done a lot of work
getting policy passed.

00:03:24.704 --> 00:03:28.666
Mexico now has requirements for
rainwater harvesting systems

00:03:28.666 --> 00:03:31.336
to be integrated into
new construction.

00:03:31.336 --> 00:03:33.421
Very fundamentally,
we need to start

00:03:33.421 --> 00:03:35.506
being able to use the
water that we have

00:03:35.506 --> 00:03:36.716
and reduce the amount of water

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:37.884
that we need to pull
out of the ground.

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:41.179
And the most direct and simplest
way to do that is to start actually

00:03:41.179 --> 00:03:44.224
using the rainwater
that falls on us.

00:03:44.224 --> 00:03:47.143
Many years ago there
was a lot of water.

00:03:47.143 --> 00:03:49.520
There were many
ditches of clean water.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.522
A lot of water would come
out from the subsoil.

00:03:51.522 --> 00:03:55.610
Here one would scratch
a meter, or half a meter

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:57.070
and the water came out.

00:03:57.070 --> 00:04:00.823
In 1970 there was
still plenty of water.

00:04:00.823 --> 00:04:04.702
The urban sprawl grew
and everything collapsed.

00:04:04.702 --> 00:04:06.412
It's all gone.

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:07.747
I would trace

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:10.166
the story of Mexico City's
water crisis to an event

00:04:10.166 --> 00:04:14.796
that happened in 1607
when the viceroy of New Spain,

00:04:14.796 --> 00:04:18.258
based out of Mexico City,
decided that Mexico City

00:04:18.258 --> 00:04:21.469
was going to be a
dryland city like Madrid

00:04:21.469 --> 00:04:25.056
and made the decision to drain
the lake of Texcoco.

00:04:25.056 --> 00:04:27.642
As the city was built on
this old lake bed,

00:04:27.642 --> 00:04:29.435
the soft ground underneath

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:32.647
would sink and the result of
channelling all of the rain

00:04:32.647 --> 00:04:35.650
out of the city as quickly
as possible to avoid flooding

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:39.320
has also stopped the process
of recharging the groundwater.

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:43.074
And that's kind of the core of
the water crisis in Mexico City.

00:04:44.158 --> 00:04:47.412
Always saving water,
always saving.

00:04:47.412 --> 00:04:50.164
We must know how to
save our water because

00:04:50.164 --> 00:04:54.335
what will it be for
our future generations?

00:04:54.335 --> 00:04:56.671
What are they going to do?

00:04:56.671 --> 00:04:59.590
We are living through this now.

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:02.552
In the future, it will
be even worse.

00:05:02.552 --> 00:05:06.597
We need more because
we are more in the planet,

00:05:06.597 --> 00:05:08.725
yet the amount of
water stays the same.

00:05:08.725 --> 00:05:12.895
We've gotten this deeply
intimate vision into how people

00:05:12.895 --> 00:05:15.648
are experiencing the water
crisis and I think

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:17.817
it's something that will
end up being relevant

00:05:17.817 --> 00:05:19.861
to many, many cities
throughout the world,

00:05:19.861 --> 00:05:23.323
what's being developed
here in Mexico City.

